  "articleBody": "Creating content for your family law firm that's both visible to AI and compliant with legal advertising rules is a delicate balance. It's easy to get caught up in the drive for visibility and overlook the critical need for accuracy and ethical communication. A common pitfall is allowing AI to generate content that includes hallucinated deadlines, jurisdictional rules, or even invent cases and attorney credentials. This isn't just bad for your firm's reputation; it can lead to serious compliance issues with state bar rules. \n\nRemember, legal advertising rules apply to all your firm's communications, including website copy, FAQs, and any AI-generated content. You can't present general information as individualized legal advice, and you must always distinguish state or county differences when discussing legal procedures. Failing to have a human lawyer review AI-assisted drafts before publication is a significant risk, especially for topics involving sensitive issues like domestic violence or child support calculations.\n\n### When Professional Guidance Helps\n\nProfessional guidance becomes absolutely essential when you're dealing with family law content. An attorney's review is critical for anything related to filing deadlines, custody standards, support calculations, emergency relief, or procedural instructions. While a qualified SEO professional can handle site architecture, local-market research, structured data, and analytics, the ultimate responsibility for the accuracy and compliance of your legal communications always rests with a lawyer. Partnering with a GEO specialist who has a defined attorney-review workflow and source controls can help you avoid mistakes like duplicating generic content across locations or using inaccurate structured data. This ensures your AI-ready content is not only effective but also ethically sound and fully compliant.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-30"
}